::ENG3_U03_L04_Q01::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Arguments: A says the town should repair the old bridge because it preserves history. B says the town should replace the bridge because the cracked boards are unsafe for school buses. What is the shared topic? {
=What the town should do about the old bridge.#Correct. Both arguments address the bridge decision.
~Whether schools should close.#School buses appear only as a safety detail.
~How to write history books.#History supports Argument A but is not the topic.
~Whether boards are made of wood.#Unsupported as the shared issue.
}

::ENG3_U03_L04_Q02::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Arguments: A says the town should repair the old bridge because it preserves history. B says the town should replace the bridge because the cracked boards are unsafe for school buses. What is Argument A's claim? {
~The bridge should be replaced.#That is Argument B's claim.
=The bridge should be repaired.#Correct. Argument A supports repair.
~The school buses should stop running.#Unsupported.
~The bridge has no history.#Argument A values its history.
}

::ENG3_U03_L04_Q03::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Arguments: A says the town should repair the old bridge because it preserves history. B says the town should replace the bridge because the cracked boards are unsafe for school buses. What is Argument B's reasoning? {
~Repair preserves history.#That is Argument A's reasoning.
~The bridge is beautiful.#Not given.
=The cracked boards create a safety risk for school buses.#Correct. This explains why Argument B supports replacement.
~The town dislikes bridges.#Unsupported.
}

::ENG3_U03_L04_Q04::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Arguments: A says the town should repair the old bridge because it preserves history. B says the town should replace the bridge because the cracked boards are unsafe for school buses. Which evaluation is strongest? {
=Argument B is stronger for immediate safety because it gives a specific risk to school buses.#Correct. It connects the claim to urgent public safety.
~Argument A is wrong because history never matters.#This is too absolute.
~Both arguments are invalid because they mention a bridge.#Mentioning a bridge does not make reasoning invalid.
~Argument B is weaker because cracked boards are always safe.#This contradicts the evidence.
}

::ENG3_U03_L04_Q05::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Arguments: A says the city should keep debate meetings short so more workers can attend after shifts. B says meetings should be longer so complex issues get enough discussion. What do both arguments value? {
~Silencing all citizens.#Both are about better civic process.
~Ending public meetings.#Both discuss how meetings should work.
=Improving public decision-making.#Correct. A values access; B values depth.
~Avoiding all complex issues.#Argument B emphasizes complex issues.
}

::ENG3_U03_L04_Q06::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Arguments: A says the city should keep debate meetings short so more workers can attend after shifts. B says meetings should be longer so complex issues get enough discussion. What is Argument A's reasoning? {
~Longer meetings always help workers.#A supports shorter meetings.
~Complex issues need no time.#That is not A's reasoning.
~Workers should avoid meetings.#A wants more workers to attend.
=Shorter meetings may increase attendance for workers with limited time.#Correct. This explains A's claim.
}

::ENG3_U03_L04_Q07::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Arguments: A says the city should keep debate meetings short so more workers can attend after shifts. B says meetings should be longer so complex issues get enough discussion. What is Argument B's reasoning? {
~Short meetings increase attendance.#That is A's reasoning.
=Complex issues require enough time for discussion.#Correct. This supports longer meetings.
~Workers should be ignored.#Unsupported.
~Meetings should be canceled.#Unsupported.
}

::ENG3_U03_L04_Q08::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Arguments: A says the city should keep debate meetings short so more workers can attend after shifts. B says meetings should be longer so complex issues get enough discussion. Which evaluation is valid? {
=Argument A is valid if the main purpose is increasing attendance.#Correct. Its reasoning connects shorter meetings to worker access.
~Argument B is invalid because complex issues exist.#That does not make it invalid.
~Both arguments are invalid because they disagree.#Arguments can disagree and still be valid.
~Argument A gives no reason at all.#A gives the worker attendance reason.
}

::ENG3_U03_L04_Q09::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Arguments: A says the city should fund a public garden because it improves neighborhood health. B says the city should fund a storm shelter because storms have displaced families twice this year. Which claim is more urgent? {
~Argument A, because gardens are always urgent.#The passage does not prove urgency.
~Both claims have no reasoning.#Both have reasons.
=Argument B, because it addresses repeated storm displacement this year.#Correct. The recent repeated harm creates urgency.
~Neither claim addresses public need.#Both address public need.
}

::ENG3_U03_L04_Q10::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Arguments: A says the city should fund a public garden because it improves neighborhood health. B says the city should fund a storm shelter because storms have displaced families twice this year. What is Argument A's reasoning? {
~Storms displaced families twice.#That is B's reasoning.
~Shelters are more useful than gardens.#That is not A's claim.
~The city should fund nothing.#Unsupported.
=A garden can improve neighborhood health.#Correct. This is A's reason.
}

::ENG3_U03_L04_Q11::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Arguments: A says the city should fund a public garden because it improves neighborhood health. B says the city should fund a storm shelter because storms have displaced families twice this year. Which evaluation avoids personal preference? {
=Argument B is more effective for immediate safety because it cites recent displacement.#Correct. This evaluates evidence and urgency.
~Argument A is better because I like gardens.#This is personal preference.
~Argument B is worse because storms are annoying.#This is vague preference.
~Both are bad because public projects are boring.#This is not reasoning.
}

::ENG3_U03_L04_Q12::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Arguments: A says students should read printed books because annotation helps focus. B says students should use digital books because search tools help find evidence quickly. What is the shared topic? {
~Whether students should stop reading.#Both arguments support reading.
=Which reading format best supports learning.#Correct. Both compare print and digital tools.
~Whether evidence is unnecessary.#B mentions finding evidence.
~Whether annotation should be banned.#A supports annotation.
}

::ENG3_U03_L04_Q13::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Arguments: A says students should read printed books because annotation helps focus. B says students should use digital books because search tools help find evidence quickly. Which statement compares reasoning accurately? {
=A focuses on focus through annotation, while B focuses on evidence access through search.#Correct. This captures both reasons.
~A and B have the same reasoning.#They support different tools for different reasons.
~A argues against focus.#A supports focus.
~B argues against evidence.#B supports evidence access.
}

::ENG3_U03_L04_Q14::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Arguments: A says students should read printed books because annotation helps focus. B says students should use digital books because search tools help find evidence quickly. Which argument is more effective for research tasks? {
~A, because printed pages are always better for every purpose.#Too absolute.
~Neither, because both mention reading.#Mentioning reading does not make them ineffective.
=B, because search tools directly support finding evidence quickly.#Correct. The reasoning fits research tasks.
~A, because search tools are mentioned in B.#This does not explain A.
}

::ENG3_U03_L04_Q15::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Arguments: A says the council should preserve the old courthouse because it teaches civic memory. B says the council should build a new courthouse because the old one lacks safe access for disabled citizens. Which reasoning is stronger for legal access? {
~A, because memory is the only civic need.#Too absolute.
=B, because safe access affects who can use the courthouse.#Correct. This reasoning directly fits legal access.
~Neither, because courthouses have no public function.#Unsupported.
~A, because old buildings never need changes.#Unsupported.
}

::ENG3_U03_L04_Q16::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Arguments: A says the council should preserve the old courthouse because it teaches civic memory. B says the council should build a new courthouse because the old one lacks safe access for disabled citizens. What is Argument A's claim? {
~The old courthouse should be replaced.#That is closer to B's claim.
~Disabled citizens should be ignored.#Unsupported.
=The old courthouse should be preserved.#Correct. A supports preservation.
~No courthouse is needed.#Unsupported.
}

::ENG3_U03_L04_Q17::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Arguments: A says the council should preserve the old courthouse because it teaches civic memory. B says the council should build a new courthouse because the old one lacks safe access for disabled citizens. Which evaluation is balanced? {
~A is invalid because history never matters.#Too absolute.
~B is invalid because access never matters.#False and unsupported.
=A has valid cultural reasoning, but B may be more effective for immediate public access.#Correct. This evaluates both arguments fairly.
~Both arguments are identical.#They differ.
}

::ENG3_U03_L04_Q18::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Arguments: A says a speaker should use emotional stories to move citizens toward action. B says a speaker should use verified evidence so citizens can judge accurately. What is the shared topic? {
=How speakers should persuade citizens.#Correct. Both discuss methods of persuasion.
~How to avoid public speaking.#Both address speaking.
~Whether citizens should stop judging.#B supports accurate judgment.
~Whether stories are illegal.#Unsupported.
}

::ENG3_U03_L04_Q19::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Arguments: A says a speaker should use emotional stories to move citizens toward action. B says a speaker should use verified evidence so citizens can judge accurately. What is B's reasoning? {
~Emotional stories always move citizens.#That is A's reasoning.
~Evidence prevents citizens from judging.#B says evidence helps judgment.
=Verified evidence helps citizens make accurate judgments.#Correct. This is B's reasoning.
~Speakers should never persuade.#Unsupported.
}

::ENG3_U03_L04_Q20::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Arguments: A says a speaker should use emotional stories to move citizens toward action. B says a speaker should use verified evidence so citizens can judge accurately. Which evaluation is strongest? {
~A is always invalid because emotions never matter.#Too absolute.
=B may be more valid for public decision-making because verified evidence supports accurate judgment.#Correct. This evaluates reasoning for a civic purpose.
~Both arguments lack claims.#Both have claims.
~B is wrong because facts are boring.#Personal preference.
}

::ENG3_U03_L04_Q21::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Arguments: A says the city should widen the market street to reduce crowding. B says the city should keep the street narrow to protect small shops from losing sidewalk space. What do the claims conflict about? {
~Whether the city has a market street.#Both accept the street exists.
=Whether widening the street is worth the tradeoff.#Correct. A values reduced crowding; B values shop space.
~Whether shops should sell nothing.#Unsupported.
~Whether markets should be banned.#Unsupported.
}

::ENG3_U03_L04_Q22::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Arguments: A says the city should widen the market street to reduce crowding. B says the city should keep the street narrow to protect small shops from losing sidewalk space. Which evidence would best strengthen Argument A? {
~A list of unrelated parks.#Irrelevant.
~A poem about shopping.#Not direct evidence.
~A statement that all streets are beautiful.#Not useful.
=Data showing frequent pedestrian congestion and safety incidents.#Correct. This supports the crowding and safety reason.
}

::ENG3_U03_L04_Q23::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Arguments: A says the city should widen the market street to reduce crowding. B says the city should keep the street narrow to protect small shops from losing sidewalk space. Which evidence would best strengthen Argument B? {
=A survey showing small shops rely on sidewalk displays for most sales.#Correct. This supports the concern about lost sidewalk space.
~A map of a different town.#Not directly relevant.
~A claim that crowding is always good.#Too broad and unsupported.
~A list of city colors.#Irrelevant.
}

::ENG3_U03_L04_Q24::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Arguments: A says all students should join debate because speaking builds confidence. B says debate should remain optional because forced public speaking may increase anxiety for some students. Which evaluation is most valid? {
=A is valid for confidence-building, but B is valid for student well-being and choice.#Correct. This fairly evaluates both reasons.
~Only A has reasoning.#B gives anxiety as a reason.
~Only B has a claim.#Both have claims.
~Both are invalid because students are mentioned.#Mentioning students does not make reasoning invalid.
}

::ENG3_U03_L04_Q25::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Arguments: A says all students should join debate because speaking builds confidence. B says debate should remain optional because forced public speaking may increase anxiety for some students. Which conclusion best compares the arguments? {
~Argument A and B are identical because both mention debate.#They differ in claims and reasoning.
~Argument A proves every student loves speaking.#It does not prove that.
=Argument A emphasizes skill growth, while Argument B emphasizes student readiness and choice.#Correct. This comparison identifies each line of reasoning.
~Argument B gives no reason for its claim.#It gives anxiety as a reason.
}
